= Problem 4.1 (The 1PI effective action)

#task[
  In the lecture, we have defined
  #tagged(1, $ tau(x_1, dots, x_n) = delta/(i delta J(x_1)) dots delta/(i delta J(x_n)) i W[J], $)
  #tagged(2, $ tG(x_1, dots, x_n) = delta/(delta phi(x_1)) dots delta/(delta phi(x_n)) i Gamma[phi]. $)
  What is the interpretation of $tau(x_1, dots, x_n)$ and $Gamma(x_1, dots, x_n)$?
  We have presented both a computational and a graphic method to relate $tau(x_1, x_2, x_3)$ and $Gamma(x_1, x_2, x_3)$. Revise these methods and use them to relate $tau(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4)$ and $Gamma(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4)$. Argue that the result demonstrates that $Gamma[phi]$ is indeed a generating functional of the 1PI-correlators.
]

In analogy to $G_J (x_1, dots, x_n) = product_(j=1)^n delta/(i delta J(x_j)) Z[J]/Z[0]$, where $Z[J]/Z[0]$ is the generating functional for all $n$-point Green's functions $G_J (x_1, dots, x_n)$ in the presence of the source $J$, $tau(x_1, dots, x_n) = G_J^((c)) (x_1, dots, x_n) = product_(j=1)^n delta/(i delta J(x_j)) i W[J]$ denotes a fully connected $n$-point Green's function in the presence of $J$ and $W[J]$ is the generating functional of connected Green's functions. The fully connected are a subset of all Green's functions. An even stronger reduction are the fully connected, amputated 1PI Green's functions $tG(x_1, dots, x_n)$ in the presence of the field $phi$ (or without: $Gamma(x_1, dots, x_n) = tG(x_1, dots, x_n)|_(phi = 0)$) generated by the 1PI effective action $Gamma[phi]$.

In the case of $n = 3$, $tau(x_1, x_2, x_3)$ and $tG(y_1, y_2, y_3)$ are related as follows,
$ tau(x_1, x_2, x_3) = integral dd(4, y_1) dd(4, y_2) dd(4, y_3) thin tau(x_1, y_1) tau(x_2, y_2) tau(x_3, y_3) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3), $
or displayed graphically
$
  tau_3 (x_1, x_2, x_3) = #diagram(blob((0, 0), $W$)) |_3 = #diagram({
    ext((-0.3, 0), $x_1$)
    draw.line((-0.1, 0), (1.3, 0))
    blob((0.6, 0), $W$)
    ext((1.6, 0), $x_2$)
    draw.line((1.8, 0), (5.6, 0))
    blob((2.5, 0), $W$)
    blob((3.5, 0), $Gamma$)
    blob((4.5, 0), $W$)
    ext((5.85, 0), $x_3$)
  })
$
or illustrated in diagrams,
$
  #diagram({
    draw.line((0, 0), (0, 1))
    draw.line((0, 0), (-0.95, -0.55))
    draw.line((0, 0), (0.95, -0.55))
    blob((0, 0), $W$, r: 0.3)
    ext((0, 1.2), $x_2$)
    ext((-1.15, -0.65), $x_1$)
    ext((1.15, -0.65), $x_3$)
  })
  quad = quad
  #diagram({
    wleg((0, 0), (0, 1.3), label: $W$)
    wleg((0, 0), (-1.15, -0.7), label: $W$)
    wleg((0, 0), (1.15, -0.7), label: $W$)
    blob((0, 0), $Gamma$, r: 0.3)
    ext((0, 1.5), $x_2$)
    ext((-1.35, -0.8), $x_1$)
    ext((1.35, -0.8), $x_3$)
  }).
$
For $n = 4$, we need to consider
$ tau(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4) = delta/(i delta J(x_4)) tau(x_1, x_2, x_3) = delta/(i delta J(x_4)) integral dd(4, y_1) dd(4, y_2) dd(4, y_3) thin tau(x_1, y_1) tau(x_2, y_2) tau(x_3, y_3) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3). $
By means of the chain rule we can rewrite the functional derivative w.r.t. $i J(x_4)$ as one w.r.t. $phi(y_4)$,
$ delta/(i delta J(x_4)) = integral dd(4, y_4) (delta overbrace(phi(y_4), = (delta W[J])/(delta J(y_4))))/(i delta J(x_4)) delta/(delta phi(y_4)) = integral dd(4, y_4) underbrace((delta^2 W[J])/(i delta J(x_4) i delta J(y_4)), tau_2 (x_4, y_4)) delta/(delta phi(y_4)) = integral dd(4, y_4) tau_2 (x_4, y_4) delta/(delta phi(y_4)). $
Applying the product rule, we get four terms:
$
  tau(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4) & = integral product_(k=1)^4 dd(4, y_k) thin tau(x_k, y_k) underbrace(delta/(delta phi(y_4)) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3), tG(y_1, y_2, y_3, y_4)) \
  & quad + integral product_(k=1)^4 dd(4, y_k) thin tau(x_1, y_1, y_4) tau(x_2, y_2) tau(x_3, y_3) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3) \
  & quad + integral product_(k=1)^4 dd(4, y_k) thin tau(x_1, y_1) tau(x_2, y_2, y_4) tau(x_3, y_3) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3) \
  & quad + integral product_(k=1)^4 dd(4, y_k) thin tau(x_1, y_1) tau(x_2, y_2) tau(x_3, y_3, y_4) tG(y_1, y_2, y_3).
$
To make better sense of the above, we may insert the expression for the case $n = 3$, i.e. $tau(x_1, x_2, x_3)$. Illustrated again in terms of diagrams, we found that
$
  #diagram(scale: 0.95, {
    for (p, l) in (((-1, 1), $x_1$), ((1, 1), $x_2$), ((1, -1), $x_3$), ((-1, -1), $x_4$)) {
      draw.line((0, 0), p)
      ext((p.at(0) * 1.2, p.at(1) * 1.2), l)
    }
    blob((0, 0), $W_4$, r: 0.32)
  })
  = #diagram(scale: 0.95, {
    for (p, l) in (((-1.3, 1.3), $x_1$), ((1.3, 1.3), $x_2$), ((1.3, -1.3), $x_3$), ((-1.3, -1.3), $x_4$)) {
      wleg((0, 0), p)
      ext((p.at(0) * 1.15, p.at(1) * 1.15), l)
    }
    blob((0, 0), $Gamma_4$, r: 0.32)
  })
  + #diagram(scale: 0.95, {
    wleg((0, 0.75), (0, -0.75))
    for (c, ps) in (((0, 0.75), (((-1.1, 1.85), $x_2$), ((1.1, 1.85), $x_1$))), ((0, -0.75), (((-1.1, -1.85), $x_4$), ((1.1, -1.85), $x_3$)))) {
      for (p, l) in ps {
        wleg(c, p)
        ext((p.at(0) * 1.15, p.at(1) + 0.15 * calc.abs(p.at(1)) / p.at(1)), l)
      }
      blob(c, $Gamma_3$, r: 0.26)
    }
  })
  + #diagram(scale: 0.95, {
    wleg((-0.75, 0), (0.75, 0))
    for (c, ps) in (((-0.75, 0), (((-1.85, 1.1), $x_1$), ((-1.85, -1.1), $x_4$))), ((0.75, 0), (((1.85, 1.1), $x_2$), ((1.85, -1.1), $x_3$)))) {
      for (p, l) in ps {
        wleg(c, p)
        ext((p.at(0) + 0.2 * calc.abs(p.at(0)) / p.at(0), p.at(1) * 1.15), l)
      }
      blob(c, $Gamma_3$, r: 0.26)
    }
  })
  + dots.c
$
This result was to be expected since it confirms for the case $n = 4$ that $tG_4$ gives indeed the amputated, fully connected 1PI 4-point Green's function. The legs merely carry corrections $W_2$ due to the fully (Dyson) resummed propagator $G_2 = #prop($W$) |_(J = 0) = #prop([], fill: luma(150))$, and the three additional terms are one-particle reducible (1PR) diagrams that can be split with a single cut at the central #diagram(scale: 0.8, blob((0, 0), $W_2$, r: 0.24)).
Therefore, the fourth derivative of $i Gamma[phi]$ w.r.t. $phi$ is exactly the 1PI 4-point function.

= Problem 4.2 (Coleman-Weinberg potential for $phi^4$-theory)

#task[
  In this exercise, we calculate the quantum effective action $Gamma[phi]$ for the $phi^4$-theory,
  #tagged(3, $ S[phi] = integral dd(4, x) (-1/2 phi(x) (partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2) phi(x) - lambda/4! phi^4 (x)), quad tilde(m)^2 = m^2 - i epsilon $)
  up to one-loop order for a constant background field $phi = phi_0$. In the lecture, it was shown that
  #tagged(4, $ Gamma[phi] = S[phi] + ħ g1[phi] + "'higher loop corrections'", quad "with" $)
  #tagged(5, $ g1[phi] = -i ln integral hat(Dm) f thin e^(1/2 f dot (-i/ħ (delta^2 S[phi])/(delta phi delta phi)) dot f), $)
  where the measure $hat(Dm)$ is defined such that $Z[0] = 1$.
]

#task(label: "a")[
  Show that for any Hermitian matrix $A$, $ln(det A) = "Tr"(ln A)$. _Hint:_ You may find it useful to perform a basis change for which $A$ becomes diagonal.
]

Any complex normal matrix (satisfying $A^dagger A = A A^dagger$) has an orthogonal eigenvector basis. Assembling these eigenvectors into the unitary matrix $U$ ($U U^dagger = U^dagger U = bb(1)$), any complex normal matrix $A$ can be decomposed
$ A = U D U^dagger, $
where $D$ is a diagonal matrix which, if $A$ is also hermitian ($A = A^dagger$), has only real values, namely the eigenvalues $lambda_i$ of $A$. Performing such a change of basis, we get
$
  ln(det A) & = ln(det(U D U^dagger)) = ln(det U dot det D dot det U^dagger) \
  & = ln(det(underbrace(U U^dagger, bb(1))) product_i lambda_i) = sum_i ln(lambda_i) = "Tr"(ln A).
$

#task(label: "b")[
  Evaluate $g1$ by means of the path integral techniques presented in assignments 2 and 3. In analogy to part a), use that as for a differential operator $D$, $ln(det D) = "Tr"(ln D)$. How do you interpret the trace? _Hint:_ The result can be found in the lecture notes.
]

$ g1[phi] = -i ln integral hat(Dm) f thin e^(1/2 f dot (-i/ħ (delta^2 S[phi])/(delta phi delta phi)) dot f), $
where $integral hat(Dm) phi := 1/Z[0] integral Dm phi = 1/(Z_0 [0] + cal(O)(phi^4)) integral Dm phi$. Since we are performing here a perturbative expansion in powers of $ħ$, where $g1[phi]$ corresponds to order $ħ^1$ whereas $cal(O)(phi^4)$ contains higher powers of $ħ$, we drop the latter and only carry along for normalization the generating functional $Z_0 [0]$, of the free theory at zero source $J$, given by
$ Z_0 [0] = integral Dm phi thin e^(i S_0 [phi]), quad "with" S_0 [phi] = S[phi]|_(lambda = 0). $
Before performing the path integral, we explicitly calculate the exponent
$
  (-i)/(2 ħ) (delta^2 S[phi])/(delta phi(x) delta phi(y)) & = (-i)/(2 ħ) underbrace(delta/(delta phi(x)) delta/(delta phi(y)) integral dd(4, x') (-1/2 phi(x') (partial_(x')^2 + tilde(m)^2) phi(x')), -(partial_y^2 + tilde(m)^2) delta^((4)) (x - y)", see eq. (5), assignment 2") - delta/(delta phi(x)) delta/(delta phi(y)) integral dd(4, x') lambda/4! phi^4 (x') \
  & = i/(2 ħ) [(partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2) delta^((4)) (x - y) + lambda/2 phi^2 (x) delta^((4)) (x - y)] =: D.
$
Now, inserting $D$ back into our expression for $g1[phi]$ and using our result from exercise 3.2 d), more specifically
$ integral Dm phi thin e^(-phi dot D dot phi) = sqrt(pi^n/(det D)), quad "for some differential operator" D, $
where $n$ is simply the dimensionality of the integral measure, we can carry out the path integration for a static background field $phi = phi_0$ to arrive at
$ g1[phi_0] = -i ln (integral Dm phi thin e^(-phi dot D dot phi))/(integral Dm phi thin e^(-phi dot D_(lambda = 0) dot phi)) |_(phi = phi_0) = -i ln sqrt(pi^n/(det D))/sqrt(pi^n/(det D_(lambda = 0))) |_(phi = phi_0) = -i/2 ln((det D_(lambda = 0))/(det D)) |_(phi = phi_0). $
Using now the expression we showed in part a), we can simplify further
$
  g1[phi_0] & = -i/2 ln((det D_(lambda = 0))/(det D)) |_(phi = phi_0) = -i/2 (ln(det D_(lambda = 0)) - ln(det D_(phi = phi_0))) \
  & = -i/2 ("Tr"(ln D_(lambda = 0)) - "Tr"(ln D_(phi = phi_0))) = -i/2 "Tr"(ln(D_(lambda = 0)/D_(phi = phi_0))) \
  & = -i/2 "Tr"(ln((i/(2 ħ) (partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2) delta(x - y))/(i/(2 ħ) (partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) delta(x - y)))) = -i/2 "Tr"(ln((partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2)/(partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2))).
$
The trace of a differential operator is given by the sum of its eigenvalues, for example a common operator trace appearing in statistical physics is
$ Z = "Tr" e^(-beta H) = sum_n chevron.l n | e^(-beta H) | n chevron.r = sum_n chevron.l n | e^(-beta E_n) | n chevron.r = sum_n e^(-beta E_n). $
A suitable eigenbasis for $partial_x^2$ is $e^(i k x)$, however since $D$'s eigenvalues are continuous rather than discrete, we need to integrate over the basis
$
  g1[phi_0] & = i/2 "Tr"(ln((partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2)/(partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2))) := integral dd(4, x) integral dd(4, k) underbrace(chevron.l k | x chevron.r, 1/(2 pi)^2 e^(i k x)) i/2 ln((partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2)/(partial_x^2 + tilde(m)^2)) underbrace(chevron.l x | k chevron.r, 1/(2 pi)^2 e^(-i k x)) \
  & = i/2 integral dd(4, x) integral (dd(4, k))/(2 pi)^4 ln((-k^2 + tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2)/(-k^2 + tilde(m)^2)) e^(i(k x - k x)) = i/2 "Vol"_(RR^(1,3)) integral (dd(4, k))/(2 pi)^4 ln((tilde(m)^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2 - k^2)/(tilde(m)^2 - k^2)),
$
where we used the Taylor series representation of the logarithm,
$ ln(partial_x^2) = -sum_(n=1)^oo (-1)^n/n (-1 + partial_x^2)^n, $
to let $partial_x^2$ act directly on its eigenstates.

#task(label: "c")[
  We define the effective potential $V(phi) = -"Vol"_(RR^(1,3)) Gamma[phi]$. Give the potential up to one loop-order, $V^"tree" (phi = phi_0) + V^"1-loop" (phi = phi_0)$.
  _Hint:_ Starting from the expression found in part b), you should find an integral representation of $V^"1-loop" (phi = phi_0)$ of the form
  #tagged(6, $ integral dd(3, k) (sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + x + delta) - sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + x)). $)
  To this end, perform the integral over $k^0$ as a suitable contour integral. Furthermore, the following integral identity might be useful
  $ integral_x^(x + delta) dd(xi) 1/xi = ln((x + delta)/x). $
  Give a physical interpretation of the two terms in eq. (6).
]

Considering definitions given in the script, i.e. eqs. (7.198)--(7.202), we deduce the following:
$
  Gamma[phi] & := S[phi] + ħ gamma[phi], quad "with" gamma[phi] = g1[phi] + cal(O)(f^3), quad "and" \
  Gamma[phi] & = -"Vol"_(RR^(1,3)) V(phi) = -"Vol"_(RR^(1,3)) (V^0 (phi) + V^"1-loop" (phi) + dots) \
  ==> V^"1-loop" (phi_0) & = -ħ/"Vol"_(RR^(1,3)) g1[phi_0] = ħ/(2 i) integral (dd(4, k))/(2 pi)^4 ln((m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2 - k^2 - i epsilon)/(m^2 - k^2 - i epsilon)).
$
Using the hinted at integral identity backwards, we can rewrite the above,
$
  V^"1-loop" (phi_0) & = (-ħ)/(2 i) integral (dd(4, k))/(2 pi)^4 integral_(m^2)^(m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) dif mu^2 1/(k^2 - mu^2 + i epsilon) \
  & = (-ħ)/(2 i) integral_(m^2)^(m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) dif mu^2 integral (dd(3, k))/(2 pi)^3 integral_C (dif k^0)/(2 pi) 1/(k^0 - sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) 1/(k^0 + sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)),
$
where $C$ is a path along the real axis closed at infinity by a half circle in the upper complex plane, thereby picking up the pole at $k^0 = -sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)$ (just above the real axis) when applying the residue theorem to perform the $k^0$-integral. We pick up the contribution,
$ "Res"(1/(k^0 + sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)), -sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) = lim_(k^0 -> -sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) (k^0 + sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) 1/(k^0 + sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) = 1, $
and no change in sign, because $C$ is positively oriented, so that
$
  V^"1-loop" (phi_0) & = (-ħ)/(2 i) integral (dd(3, k))/(2 pi)^3 integral_(m^2)^(m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) dif mu^2 1/(2 pi) 2 pi i 1/(-sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon) - sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2 - i epsilon)) \
  & attach(=, t: epsilon -> 0) ħ/4 integral (dd(3, k))/(2 pi)^3 integral_(m^2)^(m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) (dif mu^2)/sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2) \
  & = ħ/4 integral (dd(3, k))/(2 pi)^3 2 sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + mu^2) |_(m^2)^(m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) = ħ/2 integral (dd(3, k))/(2 pi)^3 (sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2) - sqrt(arrow(k)^2 + m^2)).
$
The one-loop correction is the difference of the zero-point energies $1/2 ħ omega_arrow(k)$ of all field modes in the background $phi_0$, where the effective mass is shifted to $m^2 + lambda/2 phi_0^2$, and in the free vacuum.
